                    <div class="faq-expanded__item" id="faq-4">
                        <h3 class="faq-expanded__q">How long does scrutiny assessment take in Delhi?</h3>
                        <div class="faq-expanded__a"><p>S.143(3) order must be passed within 12 months from end of AY. In practice, 2-6 months of hearings before final order. TP referral cases get additional 12 months. Patron tracks each case timeline to ensure AO adheres to deadlines.</p></div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="faq-expanded__item" id="faq-5">
                        <h3 class="faq-expanded__q">What is difference between limited and complete scrutiny?</h3>
                        <div class="faq-expanded__a"><p>Limited scrutiny examines only the specific CASS-flagged issue - AO cannot expand without PCIT approval. Complete scrutiny examines entire return. Limited is more common for Delhi taxpayers; complete is for high-value/complex cases.</p></div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="faq-expanded__item" id="faq-6">
                        <h3 class="faq-expanded__q">Can a CA represent me at scrutiny hearings?</h3>
                        <div class="faq-expanded__a"><p>Yes. A CA with valid certificate of practice can represent you as authorised representative in all proceedings - both faceless e-Proceedings and physical hearings. Patron's senior CA team regularly represents at Civic Centre, ARA Centre and ITAT Delhi.</p></div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="faq-expanded__item" id="faq-7">
                        <h3 class="faq-expanded__q">What happens after the S.143(3) assessment order?</h3>
                        <div class="faq-expanded__a"><p>If additions made: S.156 demand notice issued. Options: pay if justified, file S.154 rectification for errors, file S.246A appeal before CIT(A) within 30 days, or apply S.220(6) stay. Second appeal before ITAT Delhi at Delhigate, I.P. Estate.</p></div>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>


